From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anonymous Submission #60 <anon@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 12:57:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] RNGuard: AArch64 backend using memory tagging

Implements the RNGuard gate on AArch64 with MTE. Where the x86-64 backend
is permission-based -- the pages are simply inaccessible until the gate
opens -- MTE is match-based: the protected memory is given allocation tag
RNGUARD_TAG, and only a pointer carrying that tag can access it. An attacker
with an arbitrary write reaches the RNG's state through an ordinary,
untagged kernel pointer, and the mismatch raises a synchronous tag-check
fault.

Four pieces of arm64 configuration have to be corrected first, because in a
stock kernel none of this checks anything:

  - TCR_EL1.TCMA1 exempts tag-0xF pointers -- every ordinary kernel pointer
    -- from checking. It is set only under CONFIG_KASAN_HW_TAGS, so
    enabling KASAN_HW_TAGS silently voids this defense even though every
    register dump and ldg readback still looks correct.
  - SCTLR_EL1.TCF is likewise set only from KASAN's mte_enable_kernel_sync().
    mte_cpu_setup() now sets it directly, which covers the boot CPU,
    secondaries, hotplug and resume in one place.
  - kasan_init_hw_tags_cpu() returns early when KASAN is off, before the
    above would run; it now proceeds when RNGuard is enabled.
  - map_mem() maps the entire linear map MT_NORMAL_TAGGED. With TCMA1
    cleared and TCF on, every ordinary kernel access would be checked
    against tag-0 granules and panic. The linear map is therefore mapped
    plain PAGE_KERNEL and only the pages RNGuard actually protects are
    promoted. This costs userspace PROT_MTE, which is a deliberate
    trade-off for the research kernel and is noted in the artifact docs.

An EL1 tag-check fault now fails closed. Upstream's handler disables tag
checking on the faulting CPU and retries, which would let a single stray
write switch the defense off and then succeed -- silently, since
report_tag_fault() is an empty stub without KASAN_HW_TAGS. RNGuard panics
instead. Exception fixups run first, so the *_kernel_nofault() probes used
by the self-tests are unaffected.

Transient state is handled as on x86-64, by pivoting sp onto a per-CPU
tagged pseudo-stack (rnguard-stack.S) for the duration of each window, with
a guard page below it. The tag on sp is load-bearing rather than cosmetic:
AArch64 exempts [sp, #imm] accesses from tag checking, but not write-back
forms nor addresses the compiler materialises out of sp into a GPR, and
those are the accesses that make an attacker's untagged pointer fault. The
frame record is stored with a write-back form precisely so the first
instruction after the pivot is tag-checked.
